 "operation": [
  {
   "topic": "Variants and successor",
   "page": 1,
   "text": "The IR2153 comes in an 8-pin DIP and the IR2153S in an 8-pin SOIC; the IR2153D adds a bootstrap diode, on a separate die, and comes only in the 8-pin DIP. The diode is rated 50 ns typical. Each is offered in a standard and a lead-free (PbF) build. The family improves on the IR2155 and IR2151, and Infineon (IR) points new designs to the IRS2153D."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Oscillator",
   "page": 8,
   "text": "The front end works like a CMOS 555 timer. The RT pin drives high or low, and the resistor RT charges or discharges CT from it. When CT climbs to 2/3 of VCC (VCT+, 8.0 V typical at 12 V) RT switches low; when CT falls to 1/3 of VCC (VCT-, 4.0 V typical) RT switches high again. The RT pin duty cycle is 50 % (48 to 52 %). LO follows RT in phase and HO is its complement, each turned on a deadtime td (1.2 µs typical) after the other turns off. With RT = 36.9 kΩ and CT = 1 nF the frequency is 20 kHz; with 7.43 kΩ it is 100 kHz. RT should be at least 10 kΩ and CT at least 330 pF."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Start-up and undervoltage lockout",
   "page": 8,
   "text": "Below the falling threshold VCCUV- (8.0 V typical) the part draws only 75 µA typical from VCC, holds HO and LO low, holds RT near COM and pulls CT down with about 0.7 mA. When VCC rises past VCCUV+ (9.0 V typical) RT goes high and CT ramps up from zero; LO turns on a deadtime after CT passes 1/3 of VCC, and HO turns on a deadtime after RT first goes low at 2/3 of VCC. From there CT swings between 1/3 and 2/3 of VCC. LO and HO pulses have the same width from the first cycle once VCCUV+ is reached, so the frequency holds steadier through start-up. Lockout hysteresis is 1 V typical, which helps noise immunity."
  },
  {
   "topic": "VCC clamp and supply current",
   "page": 2,
   "text": "A zener of 15.6 V typical (14.4 to 16.8 V at 5 mA) clamps VCC to COM, so VCC is meant to be fed from a current-limited source such as a resistor from the rail, with enough current to keep the zener conducting. The current into VCC is limited to 25 mA absolute and 5 mA recommended. VCC must not come from a low-impedance DC source above VCLAMP. Operating VCC runs from 10 V up to VCLAMP."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Shutdown",
   "page": 4,
   "text": "Pulling CT below VCTSD (2.1 V typical, 1.8 to 2.4 V; about 1/6 of VCC) disables both gate outputs, so a low-voltage signal can stop the bridge, for example through a small transistor from CT to COM. In shutdown the RT pin is held high, within 10 mV typical of VCC at 100 µA. The delay from shutdown to the outputs is 660 ns typical."
  },
  {
   "topic": "High-side supply and offset",
   "page": 2,
   "text": "The high-side driver floats on VS, which may sit from -3.0 V to 600 V in steady state and slew at up to 50 V/ns either way. VBS is recommended from VCC − 0.7 V up to VCLAMP; a VBS of 4.0 V typical (5.0 V maximum) is enough for HO to follow RT. VS must not ring inductively more than 5 V below ground during switching. VBS draws 30 µA typical quiescent and nothing during lockout; leakage from the offset supply is at most 50 µA at 600 V."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Gate drive",
   "page": 5,
   "text": "Into 1000 pF the outputs rise in 80 ns and fall in 45 ns typical. Peak di/dt of the drivers is kept low for noise immunity, and the outputs swing to within 100 mV of their rails at zero current. The part also has latch-up immunity on every input and output and ESD protection on every lead."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Timing definitions",
   "page": 8,
   "text": "Rise and fall times of HO and LO are defined with the 10 % and 90 % points of the output edges, referred to the 50 % crossings of the RT edges. Deadtime is taken between the 90 % point of the output turning off and the 10 % point of the other output turning on."
  }
 ],
